      "side": 1,
      "message": "\u003e RAND_cleanup\n\nOh, hmm, that\u0027s a documentation error. `RAND_cleanup` hasn\u0027t done anything since 2015. I\u0027ll go fix that. :-)\n\n\u003e I can call one of the first two APIs to get random output generated by the PRNG or TRNG, respectively.\n\nI think you can just call `RAND_bytes` and ignore the rest.`RAND_get_system_entropy_for_custom_prng`, that was added for a fairly obscure use case and probably is not what you need. (Also it just calls the underlying `CRYPTO_sysrand` function. Since you all provide that function, you already know what it does and can call the underlying thing yourself if you really want it. No sense in adding more layers. 😊)\n\n\u003e BTW, by internalizing the seeding process, clients won\u0027t be able to reproduce the same results using the PRNG. Is this intended?\n\nYes. The point of a PRNG is to give unreproducible results. If you want reproducible results for testing purposes, that\u0027s usually better done at another layer. (In particular, fixing the seed and then, say, writing test vectors for ECDSA signing will implicitly make your test depend on the exact way that the ECDSA happens to consume entropy, but that\u0027s an implementation detail.)",
