      "writtenOn": "2023-07-10T12:18:04Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "\u003e in the end, can\u0027t we assume the TRNG to only ever be dedicated to seeding the PRNG?\n\nI would like to keep both APIs. Actually I have two use cases [1] for RNG in remote attestation, where the RNG will be called many times. I would like to use PRNG in them for efficiency. However, regarding the VM secret in pvmfw [2], since it\u0027s so critical, I recommend continuing to use the TRNG for the highest level of security.\n\n[1] https://docs.google.com/document/d/1DccVWFXY-asXyuKY4wxtSOlE3zwZYvwn4oUU629q-i0/edit?usp\u003dsharing\n[2] https://cs.android.com/android/platform/superproject/+/master:packages/modules/Virtualization/pvmfw/src/instance.rs;l\u003d143;drc\u003d7c55c7d33fec0a8363f67190c2dfffe2769396ce\n\n\u003e I find it confusing that a client only interested in random values would need to import (and initialize?) vmbase::crypto.\n\nWe can have both `vmbase::rand` and `vmbase::crypto` in that sense. But it looks like they will have common error parsing code. TBH, besides the `TRNG` code, I think we mostly need some sort of `libbssl_nostd` rust wrapper around the binding code, something like the rust wrapper of `open-dice` for example.\n\n\u003e Regarding function names, I agree that having the name `prng::seed()` is much clearer than `prng::init()` but there\u0027s more to initializing the PRNG than seeding it as the TRNG needs to be initialized.\n\nI see your point. But I want to keep the `seed` API as it is without the TRNG init part also because we may want to reseed it later. As a PRNG is a deterministic process, when it only takes entropy source once in the beginning, if state_i is compromised, all subsequent states state_j (where j \u003e i) and the random output generated from them can be derived. Thus, it may be necessary to reseed the PRNG periodically in the future to maintain a high level of security.\n\n[3] https://wiki.openssl.org/index.php/Random_Numbers \u003e Reseed",
